    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "idk it just feels like something is weirdly holding them back. like it feels almost intentional, if that makes sense? like they're doing everything right but there's this invisible wall they just keep hitting every single session. is there any physiological reason why a plateau would feel so... stubborn?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That feeling of an \"invisible wall\" is incredibly common, and it often has a very stubborn physiological basis, even if it feels almost like a deliberate barrier. When a plateau is that persistent, it usually means the body has reached a temporary adaptation limit for the *specific* stimulus you are providing.\n\nPhysiologically, the body is incredibly efficient, and once it adapts to a certain load and volume, it stops signaling for the massive adaptations needed to move past that threshold. It's not malicious; it's just optimized for the current input. To break through, you need to introduce a novel stimulus—a shock to the system—that forces the body to adapt in a completely new way, like changing the movement pattern or the tempo, rather than just adding more weight.",
      "source": "target",
